\details{
The defaults are an illustrative preview, 40 hosts for a year, not a calibrated setting: at them
the relapse rate is above its target band even with \code{NU_HYPNOZOITE_BATCH = 3}.
\code{NU_HYPNOZOITE_BATCH = 3} is the value fitted to four target bands, based on published
\emph{P. vivax} studies, at moderate transmission (\code{BITING_RATE} 0.08 with 2 mosquitoes per host and
400 hosts): section 13 of the
\href{https://shaziaruybal.github.io/malariafwd/MODEL_DESCRIPTION.html}{model description} gives the
bands and their sources, and \code{inst/validation/calibrate_nu.R} repeats the fit. Re-fit it when
transmission or scale change, since both move the relapse rate.
\code{BITING_RATE}, with \code{N_MOSQ / N_HOSTS}, sets the transmission intensity and is for each study to
choose.

Time is in days: one model tick is one day. A duration parameter \code{D} (such as
\code{MEAN_BLOOD_STAGE_DURATION} or \code{MOSQ_LIFESPAN}) is the mean of a geometric duration: the
event happens each day with probability \code{1/D}. The treatment delay (\code{TREATMENT_DELAY}) and,
under the default \code{RECRUD_DELAY_DIST = "geometric"}, the recrudescence delay
(\code{MEAN_RECRUD_DELAY}) follow the same rule from the day after the case or the suppression,
so they are at least one day; \code{EIP_DAYS} and \code{GAMETOCYTE_DELAY} are fixed delays. Rates
(\code{BITING_RATE}, \code{ALPHA_ACTIVATE}, \code{MU_HYPNOZOITE}, \code{DELTA_LATENCY}) are daily probabilities;
a mosquito bites at most once a day, so \code{BITING_RATE} times its seasonal and resurgence
multipliers is capped at 1.

Values the model cannot run are refused here, before SLiM starts, naming the parameter; the
\emph{Parameters} section below gives what each may be, and \code{\link[=param_info]{param_info()}} the same as a table. Each
value is a number, \code{TRUE}/\code{FALSE} or a string, as its default is; numbers are finite, and
strings are UTF-8 text without control characters. The tables are also in section 10 of the
\href{https://shaziaruybal.github.io/malariafwd/MODEL_DESCRIPTION.html}{model description}.

A few sizes are capped far above anything realistic: a mean of 1000 oocysts per successful
uptake (see \code{\link[=uptake_moments]{uptake_moments()}}), with \code{K_OOCYSTS} either 0 or at least 0.001;
\code{LAMBDA_SPOROZOITES} at 1000; the hypnozoite batch mean at 1e6, times its boost once the
trigger falls inside the run; the import rate at 1e6 a day; and \code{TREATMENT_DELAY} and
\code{MEAN_RECRUD_DELAY} at 1e6 days. \code{SIM_DAYS} is at most 1e9, SLiM's last tick, and a run that
reaches day 99999, the day the defaults use for "never", must move those days past its end and
cannot schedule anything on day 99999 itself. \code{MDA_TICK} is not 1 when a founder host carries a
copy of another founder's genome (\code{N_FOUNDER_LINEAGES < N_FOUNDER_HOSTS}): the copies are seeded
on day 2, so a round comes on day 2 or later. A founder-free run whose imports never establish a
parasite, by chance or because its settings let none take, is not refused here; it ends as never
started (see \code{\link[=load_run]{load_run()}}). Imports arrive on days 1 to \code{SIM_DAYS}: a pulse or an import window
set outside the run brings nothing, without a warning, which is how imports are switched off in
one arm of a grid. Each host takes at most one import a day, so a day's imports beyond \code{N_HOSTS}
never arrive. This warns when a day's fixed imports inside the run exceed \code{N_HOSTS} (\code{N_IMPORTS}
on \code{IMPORT_TICK}, \code{N_IMPORTS_PER_TICK} in the window, or both on the same day), and when
\code{IMPORT_RATE_PER_TICK} at its peak over the window, with its seasonal and resurgence multipliers
and with \code{N_IMPORTS_PER_TICK}, draws more than \code{N_HOSTS} imports on a day with probability above
0.01.

A named number (such as \code{coef(fit)["k"]}), a value wrapped in \code{I()}, a whole number carrying
floating-point noise (such as \code{1.1 * 100}, or \code{0.1 * 3 - 0.3} for 0), a number or factor given
for a string parameter, latin1 text, and spaces around the commas and dashes of \code{CHROMOSOMES} are
stored in the form SLiM is given. A number with any other class, such as a lubridate duration or
a \code{bit64::integer64}, is refused: give the number itself.

Each value is stored as its default's type and as SLiM is given it: a whole number as an integer
(a double beyond the integer range), and a double as the 15 significant digits \code{\link[=run_sim]{run_sim()}}
passes, so \code{1 + 2e-16} is 1 and a set rebuilt from a run's \code{params.tsv} is the set it used.

\strong{Editing a set.} A set edited with \verb{$<-}, \verb{[[<-} or \code{\link[=modifyList]{modifyList()}} keeps its class and is not
checked at the edit. It is checked in full when it is used: \code{\link[=run_sim]{run_sim()}} and \code{\link[=run_grid]{run_grid()}} build
every set again through \code{vivax_params()}, and \code{p <- do.call(vivax_params, p)} checks it at once.
A default taken from another parameter is set only when a set is built, so editing
\code{N_FOUNDER_HOSTS} leaves \code{N_FOUNDER_LINEAGES} as it was.
